        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 862,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 1016,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Measures whether two lists rise and fall together, using only their ORDER: rho is 1 if the larger a is, the larger b always is, -1 if the larger a, the smaller b, and near 0 if there is no consistent pattern. p is the chance of so strong a pattern if the two were unrelated.\n\nUnlike the ordinary correlation (np.corrcoef), the relationship need not be a straight line - anything that only ever goes up counts as perfect - and a single extreme value cannot dominate. Use it for relationships that are consistent but curved (effort and speed, loudness and size), for ranks and ratings, and for data with outliers.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.spearmanr\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\na:\nThe first list. Every input is compared and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nb:\nThe second list, the same length, held. At least 3 values.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nrho:\nFrom -1 to 1: how consistently the two go up and down together.\n\np:\nThe chance of a pattern this strong if they were unrelated. Sent first.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n1 to 5 against 1 4 9 16 100: always rising together, though not in a line and with a jump at the end - rho is 1, a perfect match by order. 1 to 5 against 5 3 4 1 2: mostly falling - rho -0.8.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.corrcoef measures straight-line correlation (0.80 for the first pair). scipy. linregress fits the line and judges it.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1000,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Measures whether two lists rise and fall together, using only their ORDER: rho is 1 if the larger a is, the larger b always is, -1 if the larger a, the smaller b, and near 0 if there is no consistent pattern. p is the chance of so strong a pattern if the two were unrelated.\n\nUnlike the ordinary correlation (np.corrcoef), the relationship need not be a straight line - anything that only ever goes up counts as perfect - and a single extreme value cannot dominate. Use it for relationships that are consistent but curved (effort and speed, loudness and size), for ranks and ratings, and for data with outliers.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.spearmanr\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\na:\nThe first list. Every input is compared and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nb:\nThe second list, the same length, held. At least 3 values.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nrho:\nFrom -1 to 1: how consistently the two go up and down together.\n\np:\nThe chance of a pattern this strong if they were unrelated. Sent first.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n1 to 5 against 1 4 9 16 100: always rising together, though not in a line and with a jump at the end - rho is 1, a perfect match by order. 1 to 5 against 5 3 4 1 2: mostly falling - rho -0.8.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.corrcoef measures straight-line correlation (0.80 for the first pair). scipy. linregress fits the line and judges it."
        },
